  "question": {
    "text": "Natural glucose is termed D-glucose because :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "uN0Va",
      "text": "\n$-\\mathrm{OH}$ on the second carbon is on the right side in Fischer projection\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "7IcXN",
      "text": "\n$-\\mathrm{OH}$ on the sixth carbon is on the right side in Fischer projection.\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "IQUkG",
      "text": "\n$-\\mathrm{OH}$ on the fifth carbon is on the right side in Fischer projection.\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "pBcHd",
      "text": "\nIt is dextrorotatory.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Fischer gave the prefix \"D\" to compounds whose bottom chiral has its $\\mathrm{OH}$ to the right. So natural glucose is called D-glucose or dextrose.\nStructure of D-Glucose :\n<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/znGMA_ZhQFWgxpoOlBmcuXwP4NIhbwBWAg0xSBTmvLk.original.fullsize.png\"><br>",
    "image": null
